;;; Binary.mon --- Evaluation-parametric binary tree shape

:author   Laluxx
:version  0.3.0
:keywords binary tree evaluation strict lazy recursive shape container

;;; Commentary:

 BinaryTree is a deliberate stress test of the Evaluation abstraction outside
 Sequence. The tree shape is defined exactly once:

                 T_E,A(X) = 1 + A × E X × E X.

 =BinaryTree Strict A= and =BinaryTree Lazy A= therefore differ only in the
 evaluation policy at recursive positions. There is no StrictTree, LazyTree, or
 tree-specific suspension API.

 This module is small on purpose. Its job in the research Core is to establish
 that Evaluation is genuinely shape-orthogonal before more tree algorithms are
 designed. If a future tree operation needs to inspect Strict or Lazy directly,
 that is evidence either the operation is inherently evaluation-sensitive or the
 Evaluation abstraction is missing a lawful generic operation.

;;; Construction and Functoriality

 =branch= receives callbacks rather than already-built children and delegates the
 timing decision entirely to =defer=. Under Strict both callbacks are evaluated
 while the node is constructed. Under Lazy each branch is suspended independently
 and shared according to Lazy's call-by-need semantics.

 Infinite recursive construction is evaluation-sensitive in a stronger sense. A
 value of =BinaryTree Strict A= contains both children immediately, so a generic
 recursive =repeat-tree= would attempt to construct the entire infinite tree
 before returning its root. =repeat-tree= is therefore intentionally specialized
 to Lazy. Strict infinite behavior belongs in a coalgebraic/state-machine
 representation rather than an eagerly recursive value.

 Mapping is structural and requires only Functor E. It transforms the root value
 and maps recursive mapping under E; it never asks which evaluation strategy E is.

 This is the same factorization used by Spine and is the intended pattern for
 future recursive structures: shape owns positions, Evaluation owns timing.

;;; Research Basis

 Michael Abbott, Thorsten Altenkirch, and Neil Ghani, "Containers: Constructing
 Strictly Positive Types", Theoretical Computer Science 342(1), 2005.
   https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tcs.2005.06.002

 Andreas Abel and Brigitte Pientka, "Well-founded Recursion with Copatterns and
 Sized Types", Journal of Functional Programming 26, 2016.
   https://doi.org/10.1017/S0956796816000022

 Aleš Bizjak, Hans Bugge Grathwohl, Ranald Clouston, Rasmus E. Møgelberg, and
 Lars Birkedal, "Guarded Dependent Type Theory with Coinductive Types", 2016.
   https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-662-49630-5_2

 Containers motivate factoring recursive shape from the contents of positions.
 Guarded and sized approaches explain how stronger termination/productivity
 properties may later refine recursive positions without duplicating the tree.

;;; Code:

import Control.Evaluation
import Data.Functor
import Data.Bool
import Control.Strict
import Control.Lazy

module Data.Tree.Binary
  [BinaryTree Empty Node
   empty-tree tree-empty? tree-root? tree-branches? leaf node branch repeat-tree map-tree]


data BinaryTree e a
  = Empty
  | Node a (e (BinaryTree e a)) (e (BinaryTree e a))


define empty-tree :: BinaryTree e a
  Empty


define tree-empty? :: BinaryTree e a -> Bool
  Empty -> True
  _     -> False


define tree-root? :: BinaryTree e a -> a?
  Empty -> nil
  [Node value _ _] -> value


define tree-branches? :: BinaryTree e a -> (e (BinaryTree e a), e (BinaryTree e a))?
  Empty -> nil
  [Node _ left right] -> (left, right)


define leaf :: Evaluation e => a -> BinaryTree e a
  value -> Node value
    (defer (lambda (_) Empty))
    (defer (lambda (_) Empty))


define node ::
  a -> e (BinaryTree e a) -> e (BinaryTree e a) -> BinaryTree e a
  value left right -> Node value left right


define branch :: Evaluation e =>
  a -> (() -> BinaryTree e a) -> (() -> BinaryTree e a) -> BinaryTree e a
  value left right -> Node value (defer left) (defer right)


define repeat-tree :: a -> BinaryTree Lazy a
  value ->
    branch value
      (lambda (_) (repeat-tree value))
      (lambda (_) (repeat-tree value))


define map-tree :: Functor e =>
  (a -> b) -> BinaryTree e a -> BinaryTree e b
  _ Empty -> Empty
  f [Node value left right] ->
    Node (f value)
      (map (lambda (tree) (map-tree f tree)) left)
      (map (lambda (tree) (map-tree f tree)) right)


instance Functor e => Functor (BinaryTree e)
  map f tree -> map-tree f tree


define binary-test-empty :: BinaryTree Strict Int
  empty-tree


define binary-test-tree :: BinaryTree Strict Int
  branch 1
    (lambda (_) (leaf 2))
    (lambda (_) (leaf 3))


tests
  :outer-observation:
  assert-eq (tree-empty? binary-test-empty) True "BinaryTree empty observation is total"
  assert-eq (tree-empty? binary-test-tree) False "BinaryTree node observation does not demand children"
  assert-eq (show (tree-root? binary-test-empty)) "nil" "BinaryTree empty has no root"
  assert-eq (show (tree-root? binary-test-tree)) "1" "BinaryTree root observation is evaluation-orthogonal"
  assert-eq (show (tree-root? (map-tree (lambda (x) (x + 10)) binary-test-tree))) "11" "BinaryTree Functor maps the root"
  assert-eq (show (tree-root? (repeat-tree 9))) "9" "repeat-tree returns an outer node because recursive branches are Lazy"
  :outer-observation:
